# Syntax Highlighting

`@humanspeak/svelte-markdown` ships **one engine-agnostic code renderer** and **two opt-in engines**:

| Subpath | What it gives you | Pulls in |
|---|---|---|
| `@humanspeak/svelte-markdown/extensions/highlight` | `HighlightedCode` renderer, `CodeHighlighter` type, `HIGHLIGHT_CONTEXT_KEY`, `setCodeHighlighter` | Nothing — no engine |
| `@humanspeak/svelte-markdown/extensions/shiki` | `createShikiHighlighter` (TextMate grammars, inline theme colors) | `shiki` |
| `@humanspeak/svelte-markdown/extensions/tanstack-highlight` | `createTanstackHighlighter` (hand-written scanners, semantic `th-*` classes) | `@tanstack/highlight` |

You pick an engine at setup time. The renderer, the context key, and the singleton are the same regardless of engine, so switching is a one-line factory change and two engines can coexist on one page. Both engines are synchronous, so **streaming stays enabled**. Neither is re-exported from the `extensions` barrel, so consumers who skip highlighting never need either peer installed.

The TanStack adapter supports Highlight 0.1 and 1.x. Upgrading to 1.0 requires no changes to imports, factory options, or theme setup; JavaScript and TypeScript property names that are keywords may receive corrected colors.

## Why It's a Renderer, Not a Marked Extension

KaTeX, Mermaid, alerts, and footnotes are [marked extensions](/docs/advanced/marked-extensions): they add custom token types via the `extensions` prop. Highlighting is different. A code fence is already a first-class `code` token, so there is nothing new to tokenize — the work is purely at render time. `HighlightedCode` is therefore a **drop-in replacement for the built-in `code` renderer** with the same `Props` shape (`lang`, `text`):

```svelte
<SvelteMarkdown {source} renderers={{ code: HighlightedCode }} />
```

This distinction matters for **streaming**. An async marked extension (one that returns a promise from `walkTokens`) forces `SvelteMarkdown` to disable streaming — the incremental parser can't reconcile a tail window against pending async work. Both engines here return a string immediately with no top-level `await`, so the `code` renderer never trips the `hasAsyncExtension` guard and `streaming` is never silently turned off.

## The `CodeHighlighter` Contract

Every engine factory returns the same two-method object:

```ts
interface CodeHighlighter {
    /** Synchronous. Must never throw — degrade to an escaped fallback instead. */
    highlight(code: string, lang: string): string
    /** Whether `lang` (name or alias) is registered. */
    hasLang(lang: string): boolean
}
```

That is the whole integration surface. You can implement it yourself to wrap any other highlighter (Prism, highlight.js, a server round-trip you cache) and `HighlightedCode` will use it unchanged. The one hard rule is in the comment: `highlight` sits on the streaming render path, so it must be synchronous and must never throw on a half-streamed fence.

## Choosing an Engine

| | Shiki | TanStack Highlight |
|---|---|---|
| Accuracy | TextMate grammars (the ones VS Code runs) — best for JSX/TSX, embedded languages, unusual syntax | Hand-written scanners for 25 languages — good for docs and chat, weaker on exotic code |
| Bundle (core + `ts`, `js`, `json`) | **87 KB gzip** (534 KB minified) with one theme | **4.2 KB gzip** (10 KB minified) |
| Output | Inline `style="color:…"` per token | Semantic `th-keyword`, `th-string`, … classes; no inline colors |
| Theming | Themes are data; light/dark needs dual output or re-highlight | Themes are CSS variables via `createThemeCss`; light/dark is a CSS toggle |
| Languages | 200+ grammars | 25 (`ts`, `tsx`, `js`, `jsx`, `json`, `css`, `html`, `svelte`, `vue`, `python`, `go`, `sql`, `shell`, `yaml`, `markdown`, …) |
| Streaming cost on an open fence | Re-highlights the whole block per flush; ~1–2 ms per line with the JS engine | Re-highlights the whole block per flush; well under a millisecond for typical blocks |
| Maturity | Stable | 0.1.x — the API can still move; pin your version |
| Unknown language | Escaped `<pre class="shiki-fallback">` | Engine plaintext (`<pre class="th-code th-code--plaintext">`) so it still picks up your theme; or the escaped fallback with `plaintextFallback: false` |

Sizes above were measured with esbuild (`--bundle --minify`, gzip -9) against `shiki@4.4` and `@tanstack/highlight@0.1.0`. See the [live side-by-side](/examples/highlight-engines) for per-engine highlight timings while streaming.

**Rule of thumb:** documentation sites that want exact editor colors pick Shiki. Chat and agent UIs that stream a lot of code and care about bundle size pick TanStack Highlight. Both are streaming-safe; the tradeoff is fidelity versus weight.

## Install

Each engine is an optional peer dependency — install the one you use:

```bash
pnpm add shiki                # for createShikiHighlighter
pnpm add @tanstack/highlight  # for createTanstackHighlighter
```

## Wire It Up — TanStack Highlight

Import only the languages you need (each is a separate ESM module), build a highlighter, register it, and map `HighlightedCode` to the `code` renderer. TanStack emits semantic classes only, so also emit a theme stylesheet:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import SvelteMarkdown from '@humanspeak/svelte-markdown'
    import {
        createTanstackHighlighter,
        HighlightedCode,
        setCodeHighlighter
    } from '@humanspeak/svelte-markdown/extensions/tanstack-highlight'
    import { ts } from '@tanstack/highlight/languages/ts'
    import { json } from '@tanstack/highlight/languages/json'
    import { createThemeCss } from '@tanstack/highlight/theme'
    import githubDark from '@tanstack/highlight/themes/github-dark'
    import githubLight from '@tanstack/highlight/themes/github-light'

    // Register once — every HighlightedCode instance resolves this singleton.
    setCodeHighlighter(createTanstackHighlighter({ languages: [ts, json] }))

    // Colors live in CSS variables; `darkSelector` must match how your app
    // toggles dark mode (`html.dark`, `.dark`, `[data-theme="dark"]`, …).
    const themeCss = createThemeCss({
        light: githubLight,
        dark: githubDark,
        darkSelector: 'html.dark'
    })

    const source = `
\`\`\`ts
const answer: number = 42
console.log(answer)
\`\`\`
`
</script>

<svelte:head>
    {@html `<style>${themeCss}</style>`}
</svelte:head>

<SvelteMarkdown {source} renderers={{ code: HighlightedCode }} />
```

Language **aliases** are resolved by the engine — registering `ts` also matches `typescript`. Output looks like:

```html
<pre class="th-code th-code--ts" data-language="ts"><code><span class="th-token th-keyword">const</span> answer: <span class="th-token th-type">number</span> = <span class="th-token th-number">42</span></code></pre>
```

Style the block background with `--th-background` and the base text color with `--th-token`; both come from the theme stylesheet.

### Unregistered Languages

By default an unregistered `lang` renders through the engine's own plaintext path, so the block keeps the `th-code` wrapper and your theme's background. If you would rather get the shared escaped fallback (a bare `<pre class="th-code th-code--fallback" data-lang="…">`), pass `plaintextFallback: false`:

```ts
createTanstackHighlighter({ languages: [ts], plaintextFallback: false })
```

Either way the untrusted `lang` never reaches markup unescaped: the engine normalizes unknown values to `plaintext`, and the fallback escapes it into `data-lang`.

## Wire It Up — Shiki

Import only the languages and themes you need, build a highlighter, register it, and map the renderer:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import SvelteMarkdown from '@humanspeak/svelte-markdown'
    import {
        createShikiHighlighter,
        HighlightedCode,
        setCodeHighlighter
    } from '@humanspeak/svelte-markdown/extensions/shiki'
    import js from 'shiki/langs/javascript.mjs'
    import ts from 'shiki/langs/typescript.mjs'
    import githubDark from 'shiki/themes/github-dark.mjs'

    setCodeHighlighter(
        createShikiHighlighter({ langs: [js, ts], themes: [githubDark] })
    )

    const source = `
\`\`\`ts
const answer: number = 42
console.log(answer)
\`\`\`
`
</script>

<SvelteMarkdown {source} renderers={{ code: HighlightedCode }} />
```

Language **aliases** come for free — registering `javascript` also matches `js`, `cjs`, and `mjs`. Shiki inlines theme colors per token, so no stylesheet is needed; pass every theme you want available in `themes` and pick the active one with `theme`:

```ts
createShikiHighlighter({
    langs: [ts],
    themes: [githubDark, githubLight],
    theme: 'github-light'
})
```

### Backward-Compatible Names

The `extensions/shiki` subpath still exports `ShikiCode`, `SHIKI_CONTEXT_KEY`, `setShikiHighlighter`, `getShikiHighlighter`, and the `ShikiHighlighter` type. They are aliases: `ShikiCode` **is** `HighlightedCode`, `SHIKI_CONTEXT_KEY` is the same symbol as `HIGHLIGHT_CONTEXT_KEY`, and the setters write the same singleton. Existing code keeps working; new code should use the `extensions/highlight` names. One visible change: when **no highlighter is configured at all**, the renderer's fallback `<pre>` now carries the engine-agnostic class `highlight-fallback` instead of `shiki-fallback`. The Shiki factory's own unregistered-language fallback is unchanged (`shiki-fallback`).

## Choosing How to Inject the Highlighter

`HighlightedCode` resolves its highlighter in priority order:

1. An explicit `highlighter` **prop** — only reachable if you wrap `HighlightedCode` in your own component. Use it as an escape hatch.
2. A Svelte **context** set under `HIGHLIGHT_CONTEXT_KEY` by an ancestor of `<SvelteMarkdown>`. Use this when you need per-subtree engines or themes, or SSR request isolation (a per-request highlighter on the server).
3. The module **singleton** set via `setCodeHighlighter`. The simplest default for an app with a single engine and theme.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { setContext } from 'svelte'
    import { HIGHLIGHT_CONTEXT_KEY } from '@humanspeak/svelte-markdown/extensions/highlight'
    import { createTanstackHighlighter } from '@humanspeak/svelte-markdown/extensions/tanstack-highlight'
    import { ts } from '@tanstack/highlight/languages/ts'

    setContext(HIGHLIGHT_CONTEXT_KEY, createTanstackHighlighter({ languages: [ts] }))
</script>
```

Because both engines produce the same `CodeHighlighter`, the [side-by-side demo](/examples/highlight-engines) runs Shiki in one subtree and TanStack in another with no singleton cross-talk — each pane just sets its own context.

## Unregistered Languages and Failures

Highlighting must never throw mid-stream — an exception on a partially-streamed fence would tear down the render. So for an **unregistered or empty** language, and for **any per-block highlighting failure**, every factory degrades to an escaped fallback instead of throwing:

```html
<pre class="shiki-fallback" data-lang="rust"><code>fn main() {}</code></pre>
```

The code text is HTML-escaped and the (untrusted) `lang` is emitted **only** as an escaped `data-lang` attribute — never interpolated as raw markup. Style `.shiki-fallback`, `.th-code--fallback`, and `.highlight-fallback` to match your registered-language blocks.

## Streaming Behavior

`html` inside `HighlightedCode` is `$derived` purely from `(text, lang)` and the resolved highlighter, so a **completed** fence is never re-highlighted as later prose streams in. An **open** fence is re-highlighted on every flush because its text changes; that per-flush cost is where the two engines differ most. Watch the `calls` and `last` counters in the [side-by-side demo](/examples/highlight-engines): both stop climbing the moment a block closes.

## Security — the `{@html}` Sink

`HighlightedCode` injects the highlighter's HTML string via `{@html}`, the same sink `KatexRenderer` and `MermaidRenderer` already use. The trust model is the same, and slightly stronger:

- **Code text** is tokenized and escaped by the engine (Shiki emits `&#x3C;`, TanStack emits `&lt;`), so user- or agent-supplied code content is never live markup. The fallback path independently escapes the code text.
- **The `lang` (fenced info string) is untrusted** — in this package's headline agent/LLM-streaming use case it is attacker-influenced. Shiki uses it only as a lookup key; TanStack normalizes unknown values to `plaintext`; the fallback path **escapes, never interpolates** it. Adversarial strings like `"><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` are covered by tests on every path.

Net: the `{@html}` sink only ever receives engine-generated or explicitly-escaped markup. Highlighting does not replace HTML sanitization; keep your usual [security](/docs/advanced/security) posture for the rest of the document.

## Bundle Size — Why It's Opt-in

Both engines are opt-in for the same reason: the core `SvelteMarkdown` bundle must stay engine-free, and it does — enforced by `scripts/tree-shaking.mjs` and covered on the [Tree Shaking](/docs/advanced/tree-shaking) page.

- Importing `HighlightedCode` (from any subpath) **without** building a highlighter pulls in nothing from either engine.
- The cost lands **only** when you construct a highlighter. Shiki with the JS engine, `ts`/`js`/`json`, and one theme is about **87 KB gzip**; TanStack with the same three languages is about **4.2 KB gzip**.
- The engines never leak into each other: the Shiki factory bundles nothing from TanStack and vice versa.
- Import narrowly: every extra grammar, language, or theme you import is bundled. Register only what your content uses.

## JS Engine vs. Oniguruma (Shiki only)

Shiki's default pure-JavaScript regex engine has **no WASM**, which keeps SSR trivial (the sync core server-renders fully-highlighted markup with no asset or loader concerns) — the right default for most apps. Its tradeoff is per-block cost: highlighting is roughly linear at ~1.2–1.7 ms per line, so a large block being actively streamed can exceed a single frame. For highlight-heavy, client-rendered apps, Shiki's **oniguruma-WASM** engine is ~2.5× faster per block and is still streaming-safe (the WASM loads once at setup; per-block highlighting stays synchronous) — at the cost of shipping a WASM asset and an async setup step. TanStack Highlight has no engine choice to make; it is already synchronous and dependency-free.

